# Question Words — Drill Group

Six drills behind `/practice/blocks/question-words`, listed by `index.tsx` in four phases. Every
drill uses terracotta and returns to this index via `backTo="/practice/blocks/question-words/"`.

## Why the split

Greek question words divide cleanly into two kinds, and the learner must feel the difference:

- **Invariable** — τι, πού, πότε, πώς, γιατί. One form, no agreement, nothing to decline.
- **Agreeing** — ποιος and πόσος behave like adjectives: they change with the gender, number and
  role of the noun they ask about.

The whole group is sequenced on that split. Learn the five that never change, then learn each
agreeing word twice: bare forms first, then the form welded to a noun.

## Phase 1 — Basics (`basics.tsx`)

`blocks-qw-basics` · 5 items · no categories.

| Greek | English |
|-------|---------|
| τι | what? |
| πού | where? |
| πότε | when? |
| πώς | how? |
| γιατί | why? |

Accents are load-bearing and are the reason these are drilled written, not just spoken:
πού/πώς ask a question, που/πως are unaccented connectives ("that", "which").

## Phase 2 — Which? (ποιος)

### `which-forms.tsx` — bare forms

`blocks-qw-which-forms` · 11 items · categories Doer / Target / Owner / Plural.

`configExtras` shows a paradigm before the session starts (`QuestionWordParadigm`, rows = gender,
columns = role):

| | Doer | Target | Owner | Plural |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| he-word (m) | ποιος | ποιον | ποιανού | ποιοι |
| she-word (f) | ποια | ποια | ποιανής | ποιες |
| it-word (n) | ποιο | ποιο | ποιανού | ποια |

Footnote: owner plural is ποιανών for every gender; τίνος is the colloquial stand-in for ποιανού.
Both appear in the item list (ποιανών as an item, plus the phrase ποιανού είναι).

Labels use the plain-English handles — "which? (about a he-word)", "whose? (she-word)" — never
"masculine nominative". The Doer / Target / Owner category names carry the grammar.

### `which-phrase.tsx` — ποιος + noun

`blocks-qw-which-phrase` · 15 items · same four categories, plus a `dimension` of
masculine / feminine / neuter on every item.

Forward is production: English → ποιος agreeing with its noun (ποιον καφέ, ποια μέρα, ποιο σπίτι).
Reverse is **not** self-assess — it is a single-select on gender (`GENDER_DIMENSION_OPTIONS`,
`getCorrectId` reads `form.dimension`). The learner sees the phrase and picks he-/she-/it-word,
which tests the agreement signal rather than the gloss.

Target items carry a disambiguating label ("which coffee? (you want it)") because the English is
identical to the Doer item.

## Phase 3 — How much? (πόσος)

### `how-many-forms.tsx` — bare forms

`blocks-qw-how-many-forms` · 7 items · categories "How much (sg)" / "How many (pl)".

| | How much | How many |
|---|---|---|
| he-word (m) | πόσος | πόσοι |
| she-word (f) | πόση | πόσες |
| it-word (n) | πόσο | πόσα |

Footnote: on its own, πόσο asks price or degree — πόσο κάνει;

πόσο appears twice as an item, deliberately: once as the it-word agreeing form (γάλα, ψωμί) and
once as the standalone adverb of cost/degree.

### `how-many-phrase.tsx` — πόσος + noun

`blocks-qw-how-many-phrase` · 15 items · `GENDER_PLURAL_CATEGORIES`, reverse is single-select on
gender like `which-phrase`, with an extra "Doesn't change" option for the invariable item.

Two deliberate design decisions, both recorded in the file:

- Masculine singular is the only πόσος form that visibly changes for the Target role
  (πόσος καιρός → πόσο καιρό), so Target items sit inside the masculine category rather than
  getting a category of their own.
- Genitive πόσου/πόσης is **excluded** — rare in modern speech, and it would double the item count
  for almost no production value.

πόσο κάνει is `dimension: "invariable"` with no category, so it surfaces only under "All".

## Phase 4 — Review (`review.tsx`)

`blocks-qw-review` · 23 items = BASICS (5) + WHICH_FORMS (11) + HOW_MANY_FORMS (7), re-imported
from the three forms drills rather than duplicated. Categories Basics / Which / How many.
`sessionSize={20}` so a session is a sample, not the whole set.

The phrase drills are **not** folded in — they test gender selection, a different response mode,
and mixing response modes inside one session muddies the timing signal.

Subtitle says the quiet part out loud: "mixing is harder, and sticks better".

## Notes

- `WHICH_FORMS`, `HOW_MANY_FORMS` and `BASICS` are exported so `review.tsx` can compose them.
  `WHICH_PHRASES` and `HOW_MANY_PHRASES` are exported for symmetry but currently unused elsewhere.
- Every item builds `greeklish` from `greekToPhonetic(greek)` — none are hand-written, so the typed
  answer and the displayed pronunciation cannot drift apart.
- Drill `greek` strings carry no terminal `;` — the Greek question mark is not stripped before
  phonetic comparison, so it would make correct answers fail. The "?" in the English gloss carries
  the question cue instead.
- The two paradigm tables share `components/paradigm.tsx` (`QuestionWordParadigm`); it takes a
  caption, column headers, gender rows and a footnote as children, so it handles both the 4-column
  ποιος table and the 2-column πόσος table.
